-- `<-` is legal only as a binding of a block (§2.2, §6.7, §12.2): unexpected_token at
-- 10:9 for a bind arrow in a definition body written on the `=` line, which is
-- an expression and not a block, and expected_token at 14:8 for one inside a
-- parenthesised expression, where the `)` was needed. The last three show the
-- longest match: `x <-- 1` is `<-`, `-`, `1` (17:9) and not a comment, `x <-1`
-- is `<-` and `1` (20:9) and not `<` and `-1`, and `x <-y` is `<-` and `y`
-- (23:11) and not the comparison `x < -y`, which would have parsed clean.
-- `x < -1` with the space is still a comparison; `parse/good/IfChains.beni`
-- pins that one.
f x = x <- 1


g x =
    (x <- 1)


h x = x <-- 1


i x = x <-1


j x y = x <-y
